From 7ebb25f684677614dd50c5650b36a9815751e715 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 22:40:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] windowing/gbm: HDR of more than 8 bits that the display does
 not lock onto steps the mode down for the session

A format of more than 8 bits that the parameters of nvidia-modeset made (YCbCr 4:2:2 where RGB would
stay at 8 bits, or RGB at 12 bits on GeForce GTX 16 and RTX 20: HdrMoreBits.h) is new to the display,
and one that does not lock onto it would leave HDR videos without a picture. So the display is looked
at: 2.5 s after the commit that put it out, as the depth question found it (ProbeOutputDepth(), which
reads NV_OUTPUT_LINK for the format now, and says it in its line), Kodi reads the display's SCDC through
the DDC port of the connector, on a thread of its own, as it does after a hot-plug event
(ReadScdcOf(), which gives the registers it read now). Where the clock is detected and a channel is not
locked, or a scrambled link lost its setup (or, scrambled, no clock is detected), the mode steps down a
level for the rest of the session (RGB at 12 bits to YCbCr 4:2:2, YCbCr 4:2:2 to RGB at 8 bits, as
without the setting), the parameters are written so, and the output is turned off and on so that NVKMS
sets it so at once, as for a hot-plug event (the display's answer to it is taken as an echo); the depth
is asked again, and what goes out then is looked at in the same way. The log says each step:
"HDR output: the display did not lock onto HDR in YCbCr 4:2:2 in 3840x2160@296703 (SCDC ...): for the
rest of the session this mode goes out with RGB at 8 bits, as without the setting, and the output is
turned off and on ...". After the output was turned off and on for a hot-plug event, it is looked at
again.

What cannot be told from the source is not guessed: a display that locks and shows nothing (issue 4:
the TV said clock detected, three channels locked and scrambling seen, and showed nothing at RGB 12
bits), a display that does not fill its flags in below 340 MHz, or one whose SCDC cannot be read, keep
what went out, and the log says that it could not be told. That is why RGB at 12 bits stays a choice of
the user.

Not looked at: anything the parameters did not make (NVKMS's own RGB at 10 bits, FRL, Intel, AMD),
native Dolby Vision (which takes the output), and an output that is off. Host test of HdrMoreBits.h (the
decisions of this look): 57 checks, 17 mutations caught; the files that the change touches and those
that include its headers compiled with the commands of the build (80 of 80). Not tried on hardware.
